WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
The Cloud Has a Seabed The cloud is
supported

2
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:07.040
by physical routes, landing stations,
repair systems, and human

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.280
decisions. A practical resilience brief
for organizations that need

4
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.440
to know what happens when the normal path
is

5
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.560
unavailable. Most organizations experience
the internet as a service.

6
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.900
A page loads, a payment clears, a video
call

7
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.680
begins, and the physical path disappears
from view.

8
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.000
That path is not abstract.

9
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.500
Submarine telecommunications cables form
the backbone of international digital

10
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.700
connectivity. The International Cable
Protection Committee says the global

11
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.140
network includes approximately 500 cable
systems and about 1.8

12
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.880
million kilometres of cable
infrastructure.

13
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.520
The International Telecommunication Union
describes submarine cables as carrying

14
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.900
more than 99 percent of worldwide data
traffic.

15
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.820
Source 1 Source 2 This matters because a
system

16
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.080
can be highly efficient and still have a
narrow

17
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.400
margin for failure. A company may have
cloud redundancy,

18
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.240
multiple vendors, and a reliable office
connection.

19
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.200
None of that automatically tells it how
dependent its

20
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.720
work is on particular landing points,
routes, providers, repair

21
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.420
arrangements, or regional corridors.

22
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.980
The question is not whether the internet
is about

23
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:10.660
to disappear. It is not.

24
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.640
The better question is simpler and more
useful: If

25
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.900
a critical connection became unavailable,
what would still work,

26
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.180
what would slow down, and what would the
organization

27
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.400
do next? Cable faults are routine.

28
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.460
That does not make resilience optional.

29
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.100
The ICPC reports approximately 150 to 200
submarine telecommunications

30
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.760
cable faults globally each year.

31
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.540
It also reports that roughly 70 to 80
percent

32
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.920
result from accidental human activity,
especially commercial fishing and

33
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.840
ships’ anchors. Other causes include
equipment failure, abrasion, storms,

34
00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.320
seafloor movement, landslides, and
sediment flows.

35
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.800
Source 1 That figure needs careful
handling.

36
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.620
It is a global annual count for submarine
telecommunications

37
00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.820
cables. It is not a probability that a
particular

38
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.920
company will lose service, and it does not
include

39
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.540
every kind of subsea power cable fault.

40
00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.960
The ICPC also notes that the cable network
has

41
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.260
grown substantially while the annual fault
count has remained

42
00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.740
broadly stable. On that measure, the
global fault rate

43
00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.660
per kilometre has declined as installation
and burial practices

44
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:12.140
have improved. Source 1 The important
distinction is between

45
00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.680
routine fault frequency and consequence
concentration.

46
00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.600
Most faults do not create a visible global
outage.

47
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.160
Traffic can be rerouted, and the affected
cable can

48
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.120
be repaired through the industry’s
maintenance system.

49
00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:25.580
The same ICPC factsheet says two Baltic
cables damaged

50
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.520
in November 2024 were repaired within two
weeks without

51
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:33.360
service interruption. Source 1 Routine
repair capacity is a

52
00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:36.140
resilience strength. It is not a reason to
assume

53
00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.800
that every location, customer, or service
has equal protection.

54
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.460
The consequences depend on geography,
available alternate routes, spare

55
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.040
capacity, permitting, weather, repair
access, and the importance of

56
00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.640
the affected connection. The problem is
not only the

57
00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.600
cable. It is the system around it.

58
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.820
The ITU’s 2026 report on submarine cable
resilience identifies

59
00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.460
several challenges: physical exposure,
longer repair times, geographical

60
00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.920
concentration, and the dependence of many
countries—especially small island

61
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.480
developing states and other underserved
regions—on a small number

62
00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.800
of cable systems. Its recommendations
emphasize timely deployment and

63
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.300
repair, risk monitoring, route diversity,
infrastructure redundancy, coordination,

64
00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.640
and preparedness. Source 2 The U.S.

65
00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.160
Department of State’s 2024 joint statement
makes the same

66
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.460
point from a policy perspective.

67
00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.500
Undersea cable infrastructure includes
more than the cable itself.

68
00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.460
It includes landing stations, software,
terrestrial connections, repair centres,

69
00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.580
and the vessels used for deployment,
maintenance, and repair.

70
00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.060
The statement calls for resilience,
redundancy, security, route diversity,

71
00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.880
regular risk assessment, and closer
government-industry coordination.

72
00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:41.480
Source 3 That wider definition changes how
a business

73
00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.020
should think about continuity.

74
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.660
A cable is not a standalone object.

75
00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.900
It is one part of a chain that includes:

76
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:53.460
the physical route; the landing station;
the terrestrial network

77
00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.520
on either side; the cloud or carrier
provider; the

78
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.600
organization’s DNS, identity, and
communications systems; the repair and

79
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.220
maintenance arrangements; and the people
who must decide what

80
00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:07.400
to do during degradation.

81
00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.960
A failure anywhere in that chain can
become an

82
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:14.760
operational problem even if the global
internet remains available.

83
00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.840
Why “redundant” does not always mean
“ready” Redundancy is

84
00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.180
often treated as a yes-or-no property.

85
00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.880
A company either has a backup, or it does

86
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:27.340
not. Real resilience is more demanding.

87
00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:30.760
A backup path has to be independent enough
to

88
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.720
help. It must have usable capacity.

89
00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.080
It must be reachable by the systems that
need

90
00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.260
it. The organization must know how to
switch to

91
00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.160
it. People must have practiced the switch.

92
00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:44.060
Authentication, DNS, email, payment,
support, and data-recovery procedures must

93
00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.380
still function while the primary path is
impaired.

94
00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.940
Two connections that enter the same
building through the

95
00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.960
same regional corridor may provide less
resilience than their

96
00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:56.420
labels suggest. Two cloud regions may
share a provider,

97
00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:58.700
identity dependency, or network route.

98
00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.040
A second communications application may
not help if the

99
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.520
same account, phone number, or single
sign-on system controls

100
00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.560
both. This is why the ITU emphasizes
geographical diversity

101
00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.440
and why the State Department statement
calls for route

102
00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.120
planning, alternative routes, and regular
risk assessment across the

103
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:22.200
cable lifecycle. Source 2 Source 3 The
security question

104
00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:27.120
requires restraint Recent cable incidents
have attracted attention because

105
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.740
some occurred in politically sensitive
waters and involved vessels,

106
00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.080
anchors, or routes that raised questions
about intent.

107
00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.040
Those questions are legitimate.

108
00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:37.620
They are not the same as proof.

109
00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.820
The ICPC says it does not investigate
individual incidents

110
00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.560
or speculate about whether a particular
event was accidental

111
00:05:44.927 --> 00:05:47.120
or deliberate. It also says it has no
verified

112
00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.880
data establishing state-sponsored
submarine cable sabotage since the Second

113
00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.560
World War, while acknowledging that
deliberate damage, theft, vandalism,

114
00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:57.460
and incidents with unverifiable intent
remain possible.

115
00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:01.420
Source 1 That is a useful discipline for
public

116
00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:05.600
discussion. Cable security has to address
malicious interference, but

117
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.620
the operational response to a damaged
cable begins with

118
00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:12.440
measures that also reduce ordinary
accidents: better route knowledge,

119
00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.180
careful anchoring, coordination with
marine users, monitoring, rerouting, and

120
00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:21.300
timely repair. A dramatic theory is not a
continuity

121
00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.120
plan. An organization cannot wait for a
public attribution

122
00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:28.960
decision before mapping its dependencies
or testing its fallback

123
00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.040
communications. What the cloud’s physical
layer means for AI

124
00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.820
Artificial intelligence adds another
reason to take this infrastructure

125
00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:41.040
seriously. Training, serving, and
coordinating large digital systems can

126
00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.560
move substantial data among facilities and
regions.

127
00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.840
The growth of AI does not prove that a

128
00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.280
particular cable route is unsafe, and it
does not

129
00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:50.140
mean that a cable fault will stop an AI

130
00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:53.880
service. It does mean that digital
concentration deserves attention.

131
00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.360
The State Department statement describes
the broader pattern: more

132
00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:02.540
connected communications infrastructure
creates greater dependency and introduces

133
00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:07.540
vulnerabilities. Source 3 The ITU’s
resilience recommendations focus on

134
00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.960
the same basic response: diversity,
monitoring, preparedness, and coordinated

135
00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:16.340
repair. Source 2 The practical lesson is
not to

136
00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.120
fear “the cloud.” It is to stop treating
the

137
00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:22.280
cloud as weightless. Every service has a
physical and

138
00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.600
organizational path. Responsible adoption
means understanding enough of that

139
00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:29.140
path to make good decisions when the
normal path

140
00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:34.360
is unavailable. A small organization’s
first resilience map A

141
00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:37.060
small business does not need to model the
entire

142
00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:40.300
global cable network. It does need a
short, honest

143
00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.500
inventory of what would interrupt its
work.

144
00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.660
Start with the work that cannot stop for a

145
00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:49.060
day. List the systems involved in
receiving an inquiry,

146
00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.480
accepting a payment, delivering a product,
communicating with a

147
00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:55.120
customer, and recovering the business
after an error.

148
00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.500
For each system, record: First.

149
00:07:58.500 --> 00:07:59.900
The service and its owner.

150
00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.360
Who provides it, and who inside the
business can

151
00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:04.700
make a change? Second.

152
00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.840
The dependency path. What domain, identity
provider, email account,

153
00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.380
payment processor, cloud region, or
network connection does it

154
00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:15.220
rely on? Third. The fallback.

155
00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:17.780
What is the practical alternative if the
service is

156
00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:20.540
slow or unreachable? Fourth.

157
00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.200
The switching rule. Who decides to use the
fallback,

158
00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.160
and what evidence triggers that decision?

159
00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.640
Fifth. The recovery record.

160
00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.420
What was tested, when, and what failed?

161
00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:33.320
This map is valuable even when no cable
problem

162
00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:37.420
occurs. It exposes single points of
failure, forgotten credentials,

163
00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.760
unclear ownership, and backups that exist
on paper but

164
00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:41.840
have never been used.

165
00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:46.300
What to watch next The most useful
indicators are

166
00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.260
not viral claims that a particular outage
proves an

167
00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:52.440
act of war. They are measurable changes in
resilience:

168
00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.100
whether governments and operators improve
route diversity; whether repair

169
00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:02.840
capacity and permitting become faster;
whether vulnerable regions gain

170
00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:07.800
practical alternate paths; whether
organizations test degraded operations

171
00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.360
rather than only normal uptime; whether
cable monitoring and

172
00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:17.280
incident reporting become more
transparent; and whether AI and

173
00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:21.320
cloud operators explain important
dependency and recovery assumptions

174
00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:25.300
clearly. The public record supports a
balanced conclusion.

175
00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.880
Submarine cables are critical
infrastructure.

176
00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:29.860
Faults are common enough to be routine.

177
00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:32.680
Most are accidental. The network is
resilient enough that

178
00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:34.400
many faults do not interrupt service.

179
00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:38.360
At the same time, physical exposure,
geographic concentration, and

180
00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:41.700
uneven dependence mean that resilience is
not distributed equally.

181
00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.120
The sane response is neither complacency
nor apocalypse.

182
00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:49.660
It is a dependency map, a practiced
fallback, and

183
00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:52.560
a willingness to distinguish a verified
incident from a

184
00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.500
compelling story. Beacon’s working
question Do we know what

185
00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.900
our digital work depends on, and have we
tested

186
00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:03.220
what happens when one of those
dependencies becomes unavailable?

187
00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:07.420
This is an operational question, not a
prediction about

188
00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:10.920
geopolitics. It is also a useful standard
for AI

189
00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:14.600
adoption. The more capable and
interconnected the system becomes,

190
00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.200
the more important it is to understand its
limits,

191
00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:20.340
its fallback paths, and the human
decisions around it.

